Assess at least: factual fidelity against the source model, adherence to the
brief's scope, the structure of the argument, whether each claim has enough
evidence, and whether the reader the brief names is served.

Do not comment on rhythm, word choice, sentence length or transitions. A separate
pass owns those, and a stylistic complaint raised here buys a structural rewrite
nobody asked for.

Grade each finding by what it costs to publish without fixing it:

- blocking — the article would be wrong, unsupported or in breach of the brief.
  A blocking finding must also set blocks_publication.
- major — the article would be noticeably weaker, and is publishable.
- minor — worth fixing while something else is being fixed.
- optional — a suggestion. It will not buy a revision round, so do not use it for
  anything you actually want changed.

Every finding names the passage it concerns, the evidence behind it (a source
claim id, a brief clause, or an observation about the draft itself), what would
correct it, and the category of stage that should do the correcting:
factual_gap, architecture_issue, substantive_issue, style_issue or minor_local.

State your confidence in each finding as a number between 0 and 1 that you would
defend to the author.

